  "image_name": "42c60e5ede388fb88673a8ee43e3f89e.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how circular-economy strategies can be organized and then evaluated with life-cycle assessment. On the left, resources flow to the user and various “R” strategies loop materials or products back into the system. These strategies are grouped by intent: narrowing resource use at the user stage (such as refuse, rethink, reduce), slowing product turnover by extending product life (reuse, repair, refurbish, remanufacture, repurpose), and closing loops after use through recycle and recover. The loops vary in size, suggesting different return pathways between users and resources, while a scale along the bottom indicates that these strategies can operate from product and company levels up to industrial-symbiosis and regional scales.\n\nOn the right, the diagram shows how these circular strategies are embedded in an LCA framework to identify the most effective pathways. Resource consumption enters a defined system boundary that includes resource processing, production, use, and recycling/disposal, with feedback loops between these stages. The outputs then pass into life-cycle impact assessment and interpretation, leading to environmental impact results and decision recommendations. Overall, the figure links circular strategies with a structured method for comparing their environmental effectiveness.",
